/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: IC-207 Intermediate Penetration Testing & Exploit Analysis */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["IC-207"] = {
ov:"Penetration testing validates defenses by safely emulating an attacker under authorization, then translating findings into business risk. This operational course builds the methodology: authorized scoping, reconnaissance, exploitation, post-exploitation and pivoting, and clear reporting. It is taught for authorized, legal security testing only. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"Penetration Testing Fundamentals",d:"Purpose, value, and the authorization boundary.",
obj:["Explain the purpose and value of penetration testing","Distinguish penetration testing from vulnerability scanning","Explain that authorization defines legitimate testing"],
read:["Penetration testing validates security by safely emulating a real attacker: rather than only listing vulnerabilities (scanning, IC-202), a penetration test attempts to actually exploit them to demonstrate real impact — proving that a chain of weaknesses leads to a meaningful compromise, which a scan cannot show. Its value is demonstrating true risk (this vulnerability actually lets an attacker reach the crown jewels), validating that defenses work, and finding the exploitation chains that individual-vulnerability views miss. A good penetration test tells you not just what is vulnerable but what an attacker could actually achieve.",
"The absolute foundation is authorization: penetration testing performs the same actions as an attack, so the only thing separating a legitimate test from a crime is explicit, documented authorization and a defined scope. This is the rules-of-engagement principle from BC-107 applied to offensive testing — a penetration test is authorized, scoped, and conducted under agreed rules, and acting outside that authorization (testing systems not in scope, or without permission) is unauthorized access, full stop. This course teaches penetration testing exclusively for authorized, legal engagements — pentesting, CTF competitions, and sanctioned security research — and the authorization boundary is not a formality but the line between security work and criminal activity."],
ex:"A penetration test demonstrates real risk: rather than reporting 'this server has a vulnerability,' the tester chains the vulnerability to gain a foothold, escalates privileges, moves laterally, and reaches the customer database — proving the actual business impact. This demonstrated chain, impossible for a scan to show, convinces leadership to fix what a scan's severity rating alone had not. The test's value was showing what an attacker could truly achieve.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-115","Technical guide to security testing including penetration testing."],["PTES (Penetration Testing Execution Standard)","The professional penetration testing methodology."]],
kc:[
{q:"Penetration testing differs from vulnerability scanning by:",o:["Being automated only","Actually attempting to exploit vulnerabilities to demonstrate real impact and exploitation chains","Finding fewer issues","Requiring no skill"],a:1,x:"Pentesting proves real impact by exploiting and chaining weaknesses; scanning only lists individual vulnerabilities."},
{q:"The only thing separating a legitimate penetration test from a crime is:",o:["The tools used","Explicit, documented authorization and a defined scope","The time of day","The tester's certification"],a:1,x:"Pentesting performs attack actions; authorization and scope are the line between security work and unauthorized access."},
{q:"The unique value of a penetration test is demonstrating:",o:["A long vulnerability list","What an attacker could actually achieve — the real business impact via exploitation chains","The CVSS scores","The number of ports"],a:1,x:"Showing the actual chain to meaningful compromise conveys true risk in a way individual-vulnerability views cannot."}]},
{t:"Legal Frameworks and Rules of Engagement",d:"Authorization, scope, and safe conduct.",
obj:["Describe the legal basis and documentation for authorized testing","Explain rules of engagement for penetration tests","Explain scope boundaries and their importance"],
read:["Authorized penetration testing requires legal grounding: a signed authorization (a get-out-of-jail letter, contract, or engagement agreement) from someone with authority over the systems, defining what may be tested, when, how, and by whom. This documentation is the tester's legal authority and protection — without it, the testing is unauthorized access under computer-crime law. The authorization also protects the client (bounding what the tester may do) and establishes accountability. Reputable testers never proceed without clear, signed authorization from the appropriate authority, and they refuse engagements where authorization is unclear or comes from someone lacking authority over the target.",
"Rules of engagement (ROE) define the boundaries and constraints: the exact scope (which systems, networks, applications are in and out of bounds), timing windows, prohibited actions (no denial-of-service, no destruction, no accessing certain sensitive data), escalation procedures if something goes wrong or a critical finding demands immediate disclosure, and handling of any sensitive data encountered. Scope boundaries are sacred — testing a system outside the authorized scope, even if reachable, is unauthorized. The ROE protects both the engagement's safety (avoiding disruption of production, the concern from BC-107) and its legality (staying within authorization). Professional penetration testing is disciplined, bounded, and documented precisely because the same actions, unbounded, would be criminal and dangerous."],
ex:"A tester's engagement authorizes testing a specific range of systems during defined hours, prohibits denial-of-service and accessing customer PII beyond proof of access, and defines an escalation contact for critical findings. When the tester discovers a critical vulnerability giving domain admin, the ROE's escalation procedure has them report it immediately rather than exploiting it further. When a reachable system turns out to be outside scope (a shared-hosting neighbor), they stop — testing it would be unauthorized despite being reachable. The ROE kept the test safe, legal, and professional.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-115","Rules of engagement and authorization for testing."],["Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and equivalent laws","The legal basis making authorization essential."]],
kc:[
{q:"A signed authorization for penetration testing must come from:",o:["Anyone at the company","Someone with authority over the systems being tested","The tester themselves","A random employee"],a:1,x:"Authorization is only valid from someone with authority over the target; otherwise the testing is unauthorized access."},
{q:"Testing a reachable system that is outside the authorized scope is:",o:["Fine, since it is reachable","Unauthorized — scope boundaries are sacred regardless of reachability","Encouraged for thoroughness","Required"],a:1,x:"Reachability does not confer authorization; testing outside the defined scope is unauthorized access, even if the system is accessible."},
{q:"Rules of engagement typically prohibit:",o:["All testing","Actions like denial-of-service and data destruction, and define timing, escalation, and sensitive-data handling","Using any tools","Documentation"],a:1,x:"ROE bounds the test for safety and legality — prohibiting disruptive/destructive actions and defining constraints and escalation."}]},
{t:"The Penetration Testing Methodology",d:"A structured, repeatable process.",
obj:["Describe the phases of a penetration testing methodology","Explain the value of a structured approach","Map the phases to the attack lifecycle"],
read:["Professional penetration testing follows a structured methodology rather than ad hoc hacking, ensuring thoroughness, repeatability, and safety. Standard methodologies (PTES, the NIST 800-115 process, OSSTMM) share common phases: planning and scoping (authorization, ROE), reconnaissance (gathering information about the target), scanning and enumeration (discovering systems, services, and vulnerabilities), exploitation (gaining access), post-exploitation (escalating, exploring, and demonstrating impact), and reporting (documenting findings and risk). This mirrors the attacker's lifecycle (the Kill Chain from BC-108), because emulating an attacker means following their process.",
"A structured methodology provides value beyond organization: it ensures the test covers the full attack surface rather than fixating on the first vulnerability found, it makes the test repeatable and comparable over time, and it keeps the tester within the bounds of safe, authorized conduct. The methodology also structures thinking — each phase builds on the last (reconnaissance informs scanning, scanning informs exploitation), and skipping or rushing phases produces incomplete tests. Understanding the methodology lets a tester approach any engagement systematically, and it connects the offensive perspective to the defensive frameworks (Kill Chain, ATT&CK) — the tester emulates the techniques defenders must detect, making penetration testing and defense two views of the same adversary behavior."],
ex:"A tester follows the methodology systematically: thorough reconnaissance reveals the organization's technology and exposed services; scanning and enumeration map the attack surface; the tester identifies and exploits a vulnerability for initial access; post-exploitation escalates and demonstrates reaching sensitive data; and the report documents the chain and risk. Because the process was structured, the test covered the full attack surface and produced a comprehensive, repeatable result — unlike ad hoc hacking that would have stopped at the first finding.",
refs:[["PTES and NIST SP 800-115","Penetration testing methodologies and phases."],["Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain (BC-108)","The attack lifecycle the methodology emulates."]],
kc:[
{q:"A penetration testing methodology's phases mirror:",o:["A random sequence","The attacker's lifecycle — recon, scanning, exploitation, post-exploitation, reporting","The RMF steps","A compliance audit"],a:1,x:"Emulating an attacker means following their process (like the Kill Chain), which the structured methodology captures."},
{q:"A structured methodology ensures a test:",o:["Finds only one vulnerability","Covers the full attack surface, is repeatable, and stays within safe, authorized conduct","Skips reconnaissance","Is faster and less thorough"],a:1,x:"Structure prevents fixating on the first finding, ensures comparability, and keeps the test bounded and safe."},
{q:"Penetration testing and defense are:",o:["Unrelated","Two views of the same adversary behavior — the tester emulates the techniques defenders must detect","In conflict","Identical"],a:1,x:"The tester's techniques are what defenders detect (Kill Chain/ATT&CK); offense and defense study the same adversary behavior."}]},
{t:"Reconnaissance and OSINT",d:"Gathering intelligence on the target.",
obj:["Describe reconnaissance in penetration testing","Distinguish passive and active reconnaissance","Explain OSINT for target profiling"],
read:["Reconnaissance gathers information about the target to inform the rest of the test, and it comes in two forms. Passive reconnaissance collects information without interacting with the target's systems — OSINT (BC-108): public records, DNS and certificate data, employee information from social media, exposed documents, and leaked credentials. Because it does not touch the target, passive recon is stealthy and low-risk. Active reconnaissance interacts directly with the target (probing, scanning — the next module), which is more informative but detectable. Skilled testers begin with thorough passive recon to build a picture before active engagement, exactly as sophisticated attackers do.",
"OSINT is especially powerful for target profiling, and it reveals what an attacker would learn (the dual-use lens from BC-108): the organization's technology stack (from job postings, certificates, DNS), its people (for social engineering, IC-... or spearphishing targets), its exposed attack surface (subdomains, cloud assets, forgotten systems), and leaked credentials that might grant direct access. A thorough reconnaissance phase often determines the test's success — the more the tester knows about the target, the more effectively they can find and exploit weaknesses, just as an attacker's OSINT enables their spearphishing and exploitation. Reconnaissance also has a defensive lesson: the attack surface OSINT reveals is what the organization should reduce, making the recon phase valuable feedback even before any exploitation."],
ex:"A tester's passive reconnaissance — certificate transparency logs, DNS enumeration, and breach-dump searches — reveals a forgotten subdomain pointing to an unmaintained server, the organization's technology stack, and several leaked employee credentials. This OSINT, gathered without touching the target, shapes the entire test: the forgotten server becomes a prime target and the leaked credentials a possible direct path. Thorough recon set up the test's success, exactly as an attacker's OSINT would.",
refs:[["OSINT Framework and BC-108","Open-source reconnaissance techniques."],["PTES, intelligence gathering","Reconnaissance in penetration testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"Passive reconnaissance:",o:["Directly probes the target","Collects information without interacting with the target's systems — OSINT, stealthy and low-risk","Is always detected","Requires authorization to view public data"],a:1,x:"Passive recon (OSINT) does not touch the target, making it stealthy; active recon interacts directly and is detectable."},
{q:"OSINT reconnaissance reveals:",o:["Nothing useful","The technology stack, people, exposed attack surface, and leaked credentials an attacker would find","Only the office address","Encrypted data"],a:1,x:"OSINT profiles the target's technology, people, and exposed surface — the same information an attacker uses to plan."},
{q:"A thorough reconnaissance phase:",o:["Wastes time","Often determines the test's success — the more known about the target, the more effective the testing","Is unnecessary","Should be skipped"],a:1,x:"Recon shapes the whole test; knowing the target well enables finding and exploiting weaknesses, as it does for real attackers."}]},
{t:"Scanning and Enumeration",d:"Mapping the attack surface in detail.",
obj:["Describe active scanning and enumeration","Explain identifying services, versions, and configurations","Explain safe scanning within the ROE"],
read:["Scanning and enumeration actively map the target's attack surface: port scanning discovers open ports and running services, service enumeration identifies what those services are and their versions (the fingerprinting from IC-202), and deeper enumeration extracts details — user accounts, shares, application specifics, misconfigurations — that inform exploitation. This is where the tester builds the detailed map of what is reachable and potentially vulnerable, moving from the target profile (reconnaissance) to a concrete inventory of services and their characteristics. Tools like Nmap for scanning and various enumeration tools for specific services are the workhorses.",
"Enumeration is often where tests succeed, because thorough enumeration reveals the details that turn a service into an exploitable target: the exact version with a known vulnerability, the misconfiguration that allows access, the exposed share with sensitive data, the default credentials. Patient, thorough enumeration frequently finds the way in that a superficial scan misses. Scanning must stay within the ROE (the safe-testing concern from BC-107) — aggressive scanning can disrupt fragile systems, so testers scan carefully within scope and timing constraints, and coordinate if a target is delicate. The enumeration output — the detailed service and configuration inventory — feeds vulnerability identification (next module) and exploitation, and it is where the tester's thoroughness most directly determines the test's depth."],
ex:"A tester's careful enumeration of a discovered service reveals its exact version (with a known exploit), a misconfigured setting allowing anonymous access, and an exposed administrative interface — details a quick scan missed. This thorough enumeration provided multiple potential paths to exploitation. The patient, detailed mapping of the service, staying within the ROE, is what set up the successful compromise that followed.",
refs:[["Nmap and enumeration tool documentation","Scanning and service enumeration."],["PTES, vulnerability analysis","Enumeration in penetration testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"Enumeration extracts details such as:",o:["Only open ports","Service versions, user accounts, shares, misconfigurations, and application specifics that inform exploitation","The weather","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Deeper enumeration reveals the details (versions, configs, accounts) that turn a service into an exploitable target."},
{q:"Thorough enumeration is often where tests succeed because it:",o:["Is fast","Reveals the specific details — exact vulnerable version, misconfiguration, default credentials — that a superficial scan misses","Finds fewer issues","Is unnecessary"],a:1,x:"Patient enumeration finds the exploitable details (version, config, credentials) that determine the way in."},
{q:"Scanning during a penetration test must:",o:["Be as aggressive as possible","Stay within the ROE — careful, in-scope, and timing-appropriate, since aggressive scanning can disrupt fragile systems","Ignore scope","Target anything reachable"],a:1,x:"The safe-testing concern applies; scanning is bounded by scope and timing to avoid disruption, and coordinated for delicate targets."}]},
{t:"Vulnerability Identification and Analysis",d:"From services to exploitable weaknesses.",
obj:["Explain identifying vulnerabilities from enumeration","Describe validating vulnerabilities before exploitation","Explain prioritizing exploitation paths"],
read:["With the attack surface mapped, the tester identifies which services and configurations are actually vulnerable — matching enumerated versions to known vulnerabilities, spotting misconfigurations and weak credentials, and analyzing applications for flaws. This overlaps with vulnerability scanning (IC-202) but goes deeper: the tester validates that a vulnerability is real and exploitable in this specific context (not a false positive), and thinks about how to actually use it, which requires understanding the vulnerability, not just its CVSS score. The tester also finds vulnerabilities scanners miss — logic flaws, chained weaknesses, and context-specific issues that require human analysis.",
"Prioritizing exploitation paths is a key skill: from many potential vulnerabilities, the tester chooses which to pursue based on likelihood of success, the access they would grant, and the risk of disruption. A vulnerability that gives a foothold on a key system is prioritized over one that gives trivial access to an isolated box. The tester also thinks in chains (the pentesting value from module one) — how a series of weaknesses combines to reach a meaningful objective, which individual-vulnerability analysis misses. This analytical phase, connecting the enumerated attack surface to a plan of exploitation, is where the tester's understanding of both the vulnerabilities and the target's value shapes an efficient, high-impact test rather than a scattershot attempt at everything."],
ex:"From enumeration, the tester identifies several vulnerabilities: a low-value one on an isolated system, and a chain — an exposed service with a known exploit that would grant a foothold, from which a discovered misconfiguration could escalate to a key system. The tester prioritizes the chain over the isolated finding, planning the path to a meaningful objective. Thinking in chains and prioritizing by impact focused the test on demonstrating real risk rather than exploiting trivial issues.",
refs:[["PTES, vulnerability analysis","Identifying and prioritizing vulnerabilities."],["MITRE ATT&CK","Techniques the tester will use, informing path selection."]],
kc:[
{q:"Vulnerability identification in penetration testing goes deeper than scanning by:",o:["Finding fewer issues","Validating that a vulnerability is real and exploitable in context and finding flaws scanners miss","Using no tools","Only reading CVSS scores"],a:1,x:"The tester validates exploitability in context and finds logic flaws, chains, and context-specific issues human analysis reveals."},
{q:"The tester prioritizes exploitation paths by:",o:["Alphabetical order","Likelihood of success, the access granted, and impact — favoring paths to meaningful objectives","CVSS only","Random choice"],a:1,x:"Prioritizing by success likelihood and impact focuses effort on high-value paths, not scattershot exploitation of everything."},
{q:"Thinking in chains means:",o:["Exploiting one vulnerability","Combining a series of weaknesses to reach a meaningful objective — what individual-vulnerability views miss","Ignoring vulnerabilities","Using more tools"],a:1,x:"Chaining weaknesses to reach a real objective demonstrates the true risk that separates pentesting from scanning."}]},
{t:"Exploitation Fundamentals",d:"Gaining access safely and verifiably.",
obj:["Explain exploitation and its goal","Describe using and safely verifying exploits","Explain the risks of exploitation and safe practice"],
read:["Exploitation is where the tester actually gains access by using a vulnerability — running an exploit, using stolen or default credentials, or abusing a misconfiguration to obtain a foothold. The goal is to demonstrate real access, proving the vulnerability's impact, not to cause damage. Exploitation frameworks (like Metasploit) provide vetted exploits, and the tester selects the appropriate one for the target and vulnerability. The disciplined tester verifies exploits safely — understanding what an exploit does before running it, using it in a controlled way, and avoiding exploits that risk crashing or damaging the target (the safe-testing principle).",
"Exploitation carries real risk that professional testers manage. An exploit can crash a service or system (some are unreliable), and running untrusted exploit code can itself be dangerous (an exploit from an unknown source could contain malware or behave unexpectedly). Safe practice means: understanding the exploit's behavior and reliability, testing risky exploits in a lab before production when possible, vetting exploit code from untrusted sources (never running unknown code blindly against a client's systems), avoiding destructive or high-risk exploits against fragile production systems, and coordinating per the ROE. The tester's judgment about which exploits to use and how balances demonstrating impact against the risk of disruption — the core of safe, professional exploitation. This is the intermediate exploit-analysis skill: not just running exploits, but understanding and vetting them."],
ex:"A tester finds a known exploit for a target service but, before running it against production, examines the exploit code (vetting it from an untrusted source), tests it in a lab to confirm it works reliably without crashing the service, and only then runs it against the in-scope production target during the agreed window. The disciplined vetting and testing prevented running unknown code blindly and avoided crashing the client's system — safe, professional exploitation demonstrating impact without disruption.",
refs:[["Metasploit and exploit framework documentation","Exploitation tools and safe use."],["PTES, exploitation","Gaining and verifying access safely."]],
kc:[
{q:"The goal of exploitation in a penetration test is to:",o:["Cause maximum damage","Demonstrate real access, proving the vulnerability's impact — not to damage the target","Delete data","Deploy ransomware"],a:1,x:"Exploitation proves impact by gaining access; the professional tester demonstrates risk without causing harm."},
{q:"Running exploit code from an untrusted source against a client's systems:",o:["Is always safe","Is dangerous — it must be vetted, since unknown code could contain malware or behave unexpectedly","Is required","Is faster"],a:1,x:"Untrusted exploit code can be malicious or unreliable; safe practice vets and tests it before use, never running unknown code blindly."},
{q:"Safe exploitation of a fragile production system means:",o:["Using the most aggressive exploit","Understanding the exploit's reliability, avoiding destructive/high-risk exploits, and coordinating per the ROE","Ignoring risk","Skipping verification"],a:1,x:"The tester balances demonstrating impact against disruption risk — vetting reliability and avoiding damage to production systems."}]},
{t:"Web Application Testing",d:"The most common attack surface.",
obj:["Describe common web application vulnerabilities","Explain testing for injection, broken auth, and access control","Explain the OWASP Top 10 as a guide"],
read:["Web applications are among the most common and exposed attack surfaces, and testing them is a core penetration-testing skill. The OWASP Top 10 catalogs the most critical web vulnerability categories: injection (SQL, command — user input executed as code, BC-102's Layer 7 attacks), broken authentication and session management (the session risks from BC-104), broken access control (users accessing what they should not — the most common serious web flaw), security misconfiguration, cross-site scripting (XSS), insecure deserialization, and others. Web testing systematically probes for these, combining automated tools (which find many issues) with manual testing (which finds the logic and access-control flaws automation misses).",
"Certain web vulnerabilities deserve emphasis. Injection remains critical — user input flowing into a query or command unsanitized lets an attacker execute their own code, potentially compromising the whole application and its data. Broken access control (a user changing an ID in a URL to access another user's data, or reaching an admin function without authorization) is pervasive and serious, and it requires manual testing because automation cannot always know what a user should be allowed to do. Authentication and session flaws let attackers impersonate users. Web testing exercises these systematically, and because web applications are so exposed and so often vulnerable, they are frequently the initial access vector in real intrusions — making web testing both high-value and a direct emulation of how attackers commonly get in."],
ex:"A web application test finds broken access control — changing a user ID in an API request returns another user's data, with no authorization check — a serious flaw automation flagged as low-confidence but manual testing confirmed. It also finds a SQL injection point that could extract the database. Both are the kind of high-impact web flaws (from the OWASP Top 10) that make web applications a common initial-access vector, demonstrated through systematic testing combining automated and manual techniques.",
refs:[["OWASP Top 10 and Web Security Testing Guide","Web vulnerability categories and testing methodology."],["PortSwigger Web Security Academy","Practical web application testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"Injection vulnerabilities occur when:",o:["Passwords are weak","User input flows into a query or command unsanitized, letting an attacker execute their own code","Certificates expire","Ports are open"],a:1,x:"Unsanitized input treated as code (SQL, command) is injection — a critical web flaw that can compromise the whole application."},
{q:"Broken access control requires manual testing because:",o:["It cannot be found","Automation cannot always know what a user should be allowed to do — testing what they can improperly access needs human judgment","It does not exist","Scanners find it perfectly"],a:1,x:"Access-control logic (who should access what) requires human understanding; automation misses many such flaws."},
{q:"Web applications are a common initial-access vector because they are:",o:["Never vulnerable","So exposed and so often vulnerable that they are frequently how attackers first get in","Always internal","Fully automated"],a:1,x:"Exposed, frequently-vulnerable web applications are a top real-world initial-access vector, making web testing high-value."}]},
{t:"Password and Credential Attacks",d:"Exploiting the weakest authentication.",
obj:["Describe password attack techniques","Explain credential access in penetration testing","Connect credential attacks to defensive controls"],
read:["Credentials are a primary target because compromised credentials grant legitimate-looking access (the identity attacks from BC-104). Penetration testers use several techniques: password guessing and spraying (trying common passwords across accounts, evading lockout — the spraying pattern from BC-104), brute-forcing (exhaustively trying passwords, feasible against weak hashes or weak passwords), cracking captured password hashes offline (using the fact that weak hashing and weak passwords crack quickly — BC-104's storage lesson), and reusing credentials found via OSINT (leaked breach data) or harvested during the test. Default and reused credentials are frequently the easiest path in.",
"Credential access during a test connects offense and defense directly. The tester who dumps and cracks hashes, or harvests credentials from a compromised host (the LSASS dumping from BC-103), is demonstrating exactly the pass-the-hash and credential-reuse attacks that defenders must prevent — validating whether the defensive controls (strong hashing, MFA, credential protection, least privilege) actually work. A test that easily cracks passwords or reuses harvested credentials to move laterally is demonstrating real, exploitable weaknesses in the organization's identity security. The findings map directly to defensive fixes: enforce strong/phishing-resistant MFA, protect credentials (Credential Guard), use strong hashing, eliminate default and reused credentials. Credential attacks are thus both a common exploitation path and a direct test of the identity defenses from BC-104."],
ex:"During a test, the tester harvests password hashes from a compromised host and finds several crack quickly (weak passwords, or a fast hash), then reuses a cracked credential to authenticate to another system (demonstrating pass-the-hash-style lateral movement). This validates a real weakness — the organization's credentials and hashing are weak enough to exploit. The findings map directly to defensive fixes: MFA, strong hashing, and credential protection, connecting the offensive demonstration to the BC-104 defenses.",
refs:[["Hashcat/John the Ripper documentation","Password cracking techniques."],["MITRE ATT&CK, Credential Access","Credential attack techniques and defenses."]],
kc:[
{q:"Password spraying evades account lockout by:",o:["Guessing one account many times","Trying a few common passwords across many accounts, keeping per-account attempts low","Cracking hashes","Using MFA"],a:1,x:"Spreading attempts across accounts stays under lockout thresholds — the spraying pattern from BC-104, used offensively."},
{q:"A tester who dumps and cracks password hashes is demonstrating:",o:["A theoretical risk","The real pass-the-hash and credential-reuse attacks defenders must prevent, validating identity controls","A denial-of-service","Nothing exploitable"],a:1,x:"Credential harvesting and cracking directly test whether the organization's identity defenses (hashing, MFA, credential protection) hold."},
{q:"Credential attack findings map directly to which defenses?",o:["Physical locks","Strong/phishing-resistant MFA, credential protection, strong hashing, and eliminating default/reused credentials","Faster networks","More servers"],a:1,x:"The offensive findings translate to the BC-104 identity defenses that would have prevented the demonstrated compromise."}]},
{t:"Post-Exploitation and Privilege Escalation",d:"From foothold to control.",
obj:["Explain post-exploitation goals","Describe privilege escalation techniques","Explain demonstrating impact responsibly"],
read:["Initial access is rarely the objective — post-exploitation is where the tester demonstrates real impact by expanding from a foothold. The first goal is usually privilege escalation: turning a low-privilege foothold into administrative or system control, using the escalation techniques from earlier courses (misconfigurations, over-permissive sudo/services, exploitable privileged processes, credential harvesting — BC-103, BC-104). A local foothold with escalation to admin, then to domain admin, demonstrates the full compromise that individual vulnerabilities do not. Post-exploitation also includes establishing persistence (to survive, though testers document rather than truly persist), gathering information, and identifying paths to the objectives.",
"Demonstrating impact responsibly is the professional discipline. The tester's job is to prove what an attacker could achieve — reaching the crown jewels, accessing sensitive data, gaining domain control — but to do so in a controlled, documented way that proves the access without causing harm or unnecessary exposure. Proof of access (a screenshot, a specific file's existence) demonstrates the impact without exfiltrating real sensitive data; the ROE defines how far to go. The tester documents the full chain — how the foothold became domain admin, how domain admin reached the objective — because that chain is the demonstrated risk that convinces leadership and maps to defensive fixes (the segmentation, least privilege, and monitoring that would break the chain, from BC-101 and BC-104). Post-exploitation is where penetration testing delivers its unique value: showing not just vulnerability but consequence."],
ex:"From a low-privilege web-server foothold, the tester escalates to local admin via a misconfiguration, harvests a cached domain-admin credential (BC-103's warning), and uses it to reach the domain controller — demonstrating full domain compromise from a single web vulnerability. They prove access to the objective with a screenshot rather than exfiltrating data, and document the entire chain. This demonstrated consequence — the real impact of the initial foothold — is the pentest's unique value, mapping directly to the segmentation and credential-protection fixes that would break the chain.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Privilege Escalation and Post-Exploitation","Escalation and post-exploitation techniques."],["PTES, post-exploitation","Demonstrating impact responsibly."]],
kc:[
{q:"The first goal of post-exploitation is usually:",o:["Deleting data","Privilege escalation — turning a low-privilege foothold into administrative or system control","Leaving immediately","Encrypting files"],a:1,x:"Escalating from a foothold to admin/domain-admin demonstrates the full compromise that individual vulnerabilities do not."},
{q:"Demonstrating impact responsibly means:",o:["Exfiltrating all sensitive data","Proving access (e.g., a screenshot) without causing harm or unnecessary exposure, per the ROE","Deploying ransomware","Damaging systems"],a:1,x:"The tester proves what an attacker could achieve in a controlled, documented way — proof of access, not real data exfiltration."},
{q:"Documenting the full exploitation chain is valuable because:",o:["It is required paperwork","The chain is the demonstrated risk that convinces leadership and maps to defensive fixes (segmentation, least privilege)","It slows the test","It hides findings"],a:1,x:"The chain from foothold to objective shows real consequence and points directly to the controls that would break it."}]},
{t:"Lateral Movement and Pivoting",d:"Traversing the network like an attacker.",
obj:["Explain lateral movement and pivoting","Describe techniques for moving between systems","Connect lateral movement to segmentation defenses"],
read:["Real objectives are often not on the initially-compromised system, so the tester moves laterally — traversing from the foothold to other systems toward the objective, exactly as attackers do (the lateral movement from BC-102, BC-104). Techniques include reusing harvested credentials (pass-the-hash/ticket), exploiting trust relationships, abusing legitimate remote-access tools (PsExec, RDP, SSH — living-off-the-land), and pivoting through compromised systems to reach networks not directly accessible (using a compromised host as a gateway into a segmented network). Lateral movement is how a single foothold becomes a broad compromise.",
"Lateral movement directly tests the segmentation and containment defenses from throughout this program. A test that moves freely from a compromised workstation to critical servers demonstrates that the network is flat or under-segmented (the exposure that turned local footholds into total ones in famous breaches — BC-101). A test that is blocked or slowed by segmentation, credential protection, and monitoring demonstrates that those defenses work. The tester's ability to pivot and move laterally maps directly to defensive fixes: segmentation to constrain movement (BC-102, IC-210), credential protection to prevent reuse (BC-104), and monitoring to detect the lateral movement (BC-102's hunting vectors). Pivoting to reach segmented networks specifically tests whether segmentation actually isolates, making lateral-movement testing a direct validation of the containment architecture that limits blast radius."],
ex:"From the web-server foothold, the tester uses harvested credentials to move laterally to a file server, then pivots through it to reach a segmented database network not directly accessible from the foothold — reaching the objective. The free lateral movement demonstrated the network was under-segmented and credentials were reusable. The findings map directly to fixes: segmentation to block the movement and credential protection to prevent the reuse — validating (by their absence) the containment defenses that would have stopped the pivot.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Lateral Movement","Lateral movement and pivoting techniques."],["PTES, post-exploitation and pivoting","Traversing networks in a test."]],
kc:[
{q:"Lateral movement in a penetration test means:",o:["Staying on one system","Traversing from the foothold to other systems toward the objective, as attackers do","Only scanning","Reporting"],a:1,x:"Lateral movement turns a single foothold into a broad compromise, reaching objectives on other systems."},
{q:"A test that moves freely from a workstation to critical servers demonstrates:",o:["Strong security","A flat or under-segmented network — the exposure that turns local footholds into total ones","Good segmentation","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Free lateral movement reveals inadequate segmentation, the weakness that made famous breaches total compromises."},
{q:"Lateral-movement testing directly validates which defense?",o:["Physical locks","Segmentation and containment — whether the network actually isolates and limits blast radius","Email filtering","Backups"],a:1,x:"Pivoting tests whether segmentation isolates; blocked movement demonstrates the containment architecture works (BC-102, IC-210)."}]},
{t:"Reporting and Business Risk",d:"Turning technical findings into decisions.",
obj:["Explain the penetration test report's purpose and audience","Describe translating findings into business risk","Explain actionable remediation guidance"],
read:["The report is the deliverable that makes a penetration test valuable — a test that finds critical issues but reports them poorly achieves little. The report serves two audiences (like the SAR from BC-107): an executive summary that translates the findings into business risk for leadership (an attacker could reach the customer database and here is what that means for the business), and technical detail for the teams who will fix the issues (the specific vulnerabilities, how they were exploited, and how to remediate). Translating technical findings into business risk is the crucial skill — leadership acts on risk to the mission and business, not on CVSS scores or technical jargon (the risk-translation discipline from BC-101 and BC-107).",
"A good report presents the demonstrated attack chains (the unique pentest value — how a foothold became domain admin and reached the objective), the risk each represents, and clear, actionable, prioritized remediation guidance. Findings are prioritized by real risk (IC-202's risk-based prioritization), and remediation guidance is specific enough to act on. The report also acknowledges what was and was not tested (scope), and — professionally — presents findings objectively without exaggeration or blame. The report is where the test's technical work becomes organizational action: a clear demonstration of real risk with actionable fixes drives remediation, while a jargon-filled dump of findings gets shelved. This is the culmination of the test, and its quality determines whether the engagement improves security."],
ex:"The report leads with a business-risk executive summary — 'a low-privilege web vulnerability allowed full domain compromise and access to all customer data; the primary causes are inadequate segmentation and credential protection' — followed by the demonstrated attack chain and specific, prioritized remediation. Leadership grasps the risk and funds the fixes; the technical teams have exactly what they need to remediate. The clear risk translation and actionable guidance turned the test into organizational action, the report's whole purpose.",
refs:[["PTES, reporting","Penetration test report structure and audiences."],["NIST SP 800-115","Reporting and risk translation."]],
kc:[
{q:"The penetration test report serves:",o:["Only the tester","Two audiences — an executive summary translating findings to business risk, and technical detail for remediation","No one","Only auditors"],a:1,x:"Like the SAR, the report must reach both leadership (business risk) and the technical teams (specific findings and fixes)."},
{q:"Translating findings into business risk is crucial because:",o:["It sounds better","Leadership acts on risk to the mission and business, not on CVSS scores or technical jargon","It is required","It hides technical detail"],a:1,x:"The risk-translation discipline (BC-101/107) turns technical findings into the mission-risk terms leadership actually decides on."},
{q:"A penetration test that finds critical issues but reports them poorly:",o:["Is still fully valuable","Achieves little — the report is where technical work becomes organizational action","Is best practice","Needs no report"],a:1,x:"A jargon dump gets shelved; a clear risk demonstration with actionable fixes drives the remediation that makes the test valuable."}]},
{t:"Exploit Analysis and Development Basics",d:"Understanding how exploits work.",
obj:["Explain the value of understanding exploit internals","Describe common vulnerability classes that lead to exploits","Explain analyzing and adapting exploits safely"],
read:["Intermediate penetration testing moves beyond running pre-built exploits to understanding how they work, which is what lets a tester analyze, adapt, and troubleshoot exploits rather than being limited to whatever runs out of the box. Common vulnerability classes lead to exploitation: memory-corruption flaws (buffer overflows, use-after-free — where an attacker overwrites memory to control execution), injection flaws (module eight), insecure deserialization, and logic flaws. Understanding the class of vulnerability and the mechanism of the exploit — what it does to the target, why it works, what could make it fail — elevates the tester from tool-operator to analyst, able to reason about exploits rather than merely launch them.",
"This analytical capability matters practically. An exploit may need adaptation to work against a specific target (different offsets, versions, or configurations), and understanding it lets the tester make those adjustments safely rather than blindly trying variants. It also informs the safe-verification discipline (understanding what an exploit does before running it prevents surprises) and the vetting of untrusted exploit code (recognizing what code does before running it). While full exploit development is an advanced specialty (and reverse engineering is AC-307), the intermediate tester should understand exploit internals well enough to analyze reliability, adapt to targets, and reason about impact — the difference between a tester who can only run automated exploits and one who understands what they are doing. This understanding, applied only in authorized testing, is what makes exploit analysis a professional skill rather than script-kiddie tool use."],
ex:"A tester finds a public exploit for a target's exact software but it fails — the offsets are for a different version. Because the tester understands the exploit's mechanism (a buffer overflow with version-specific offsets), they analyze the target's version, adjust the offsets, and get it working reliably in a lab before use. Understanding the exploit's internals, rather than blindly trying variants, enabled the safe, successful adaptation — the analytical skill that distinguishes an intermediate tester.",
refs:[["The Shellcoder's Handbook / exploit development resources","Understanding vulnerability classes and exploit mechanics."],["NIST SP 800-115 and PTES","Exploit analysis in professional testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"Understanding how exploits work (vs. just running them) lets a tester:",o:["Nothing extra","Analyze, adapt, and troubleshoot exploits rather than being limited to what runs out of the box","Skip authorization","Cause more damage"],a:1,x:"Understanding internals elevates the tester from tool-operator to analyst, able to adapt exploits to specific targets safely."},
{q:"A public exploit fails against a target because the offsets are for a different version. Understanding it lets the tester:",o:["Give up","Analyze the target version and safely adjust the offsets rather than blindly trying variants","Run it repeatedly","Ignore the target"],a:1,x:"Understanding the exploit mechanism enables informed adaptation to the specific target, done safely in a lab first."},
{q:"Understanding exploit internals also supports:",o:["Nothing","Safe verification and vetting untrusted exploit code — recognizing what code does before running it","Skipping testing","Faster scanning"],a:1,x:"Knowing what an exploit does prevents surprises and enables vetting untrusted code — the safe-verification discipline."}]},
{t:"Defense Evasion in Authorized Testing",d:"Testing whether defenses actually detect.",
obj:["Explain why testers may emulate evasion","Describe how evasion tests detection capabilities","Explain the scope and ethics of evasion in engagements"],
read:["Real adversaries evade defenses (the living-off-the-land and defense-evasion techniques from BC-103, BC-108), so an authorized test that only uses noisy, easily-detected techniques does not validate whether the organization can catch a capable adversary. When the engagement scope calls for it, testers emulate evasion — using techniques that avoid or bypass detection (obfuscation, living-off-the-land, timing, avoiding known signatures) — specifically to test whether the defenses (EDR, monitoring, hunting) actually detect a stealthy attacker. This directly validates the detection and response capabilities that everything after prevention depends on (BC-101's resilience argument).",
"Evasion testing is powerful but bounded by scope and ethics. Whether to emulate evasion depends on the engagement's goals: a test aimed at finding vulnerabilities may be openly noisy, while a test aimed at validating detection (closer to red teaming, AC-303) deliberately evades to see what the blue team catches. The purple-team model (IC-205) pairs this with the defenders to maximize learning. The findings are especially valuable — a test that evades detection entirely reveals a critical gap in the detection capability, mapping directly to detection-engineering fixes (the hunt-to-detection loop from IC-205). Evasion in testing is always within authorization and scope, and its purpose is defensive: revealing what a real evasive adversary could do undetected, so the organization can close the detection gaps. This connects intermediate penetration testing to the advanced red-teaming and adversary-emulation discipline of AC-303, where evasion is central."],
ex:"An engagement scoped to test detection has the tester emulate a stealthy adversary — living-off-the-land techniques, avoiding known signatures, careful timing. The tester reaches the objective without triggering any alert, revealing that the organization's detection has a critical gap against evasive techniques. This finding — far more valuable than a noisy test that would have been caught — maps directly to detection-engineering fixes, validating (by their absence) the detection capabilities that a resilient defense requires.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Defense Evasion","Evasion techniques emulated in testing."],["Red teaming and purple teaming methodology (AC-303, IC-205)","Evasion to validate detection."]],
kc:[
{q:"Testers emulate evasion in authorized engagements to:",o:["Cause more damage","Test whether defenses (EDR, monitoring, hunting) actually detect a stealthy, capable adversary","Avoid authorization","Break the law"],a:1,x:"A test using only noisy techniques does not validate detection; evasion tests whether a capable adversary would be caught."},
{q:"A test that evades detection entirely reveals:",o:["Perfect security","A critical detection gap — mapping directly to detection-engineering fixes","Nothing useful","That evasion is impossible"],a:1,x:"Undetected evasion exposes a gap in detection capability, the finding that drives detection improvement (the hunt-to-detection loop)."},
{q:"Whether to emulate evasion depends on:",o:["The tester's preference","The engagement's goals — vulnerability-finding may be noisy, but detection-validation deliberately evades, always within scope","Nothing","Random choice"],a:1,x:"Evasion is scoped to the engagement's purpose (detection validation vs. vulnerability finding), always within authorization."}]},
{t:"Penetration Testing Capstone",d:"Running an authorized end-to-end engagement.",
obj:["Sequence a full penetration test from authorization to report","Integrate the methodology phases into a coherent engagement","Judge a test by demonstrated risk and actionable reporting"],
read:["This capstone runs an authorized penetration test end to end. It begins with authorization and ROE (modules one, two) — the non-negotiable foundation. Reconnaissance and OSINT profile the target (module four); scanning and enumeration map the attack surface (module five); vulnerability identification and prioritization plan the exploitation (module six); exploitation gains a foothold safely (modules seven, eight, nine, ten); post-exploitation escalates and lateral movement pivots toward the objective (modules eleven, twelve), demonstrating real impact responsibly; and the report translates the demonstrated chain into business risk with actionable remediation (module thirteen). Each phase builds on the last, from authorization to the deliverable that drives fixes.",
"The integration is the point: a penetration test is a disciplined, authorized, end-to-end emulation of an attacker, and its value comes from the whole process — thorough recon and enumeration set up successful exploitation, post-exploitation and lateral movement demonstrate real consequence, and clear reporting turns it into action. Skipping or rushing phases (weak recon, no chaining, poor reporting) produces a shallow test. And throughout, authorization and safe conduct bound everything — the test emulates an attacker without becoming one, staying legal, in-scope, and non-destructive.",
"The graded deliverable — conducting an authorized penetration test against a multi-machine lab and compiling a technical finding report — is exactly this end-to-end engagement. Its quality is judged by whether it demonstrated real, chained risk (not just a vulnerability list), stayed within authorization and safe conduct, and produced a report that translates findings into business risk with actionable remediation. A penetration test done well validates defenses, demonstrates true risk, and drives improvement; done poorly, it is either a dangerous overreach or a shallow scan with a fancy name — the difference this course exists to build, always within the bounds of authorized, legal testing.",],
ex:"A tester runs the full authorized engagement against a lab: signed authorization and ROE, thorough recon and enumeration, a validated exploitation chain from web foothold through privilege escalation and lateral movement to the objective database, all conducted safely and in-scope, and a report translating the chain into business risk with prioritized fixes. The engagement demonstrated real, chained risk, stayed within authorization, and produced actionable reporting — the marks of a professional penetration test that validates defenses and drives improvement.",
refs:[["PTES and NIST SP 800-115","The full penetration testing lifecycle."],["OSSTMM and OWASP testing guides","Comprehensive testing methodology."]],
kc:[
{q:"A penetration test's value comes from:",o:["A single exploit","The whole disciplined process — recon, enumeration, exploitation, post-exploitation, lateral movement, and clear reporting","Finding one vulnerability","Automated scanning alone"],a:1,x:"The end-to-end process demonstrates real chained risk and drives action; skipping phases produces a shallow test."},
{q:"Throughout the engagement, what bounds everything?",o:["The tester's mood","Authorization and safe conduct — emulating an attacker without becoming one, staying legal, in-scope, and non-destructive","The number of tools","The report length"],a:1,x:"Authorization and safe, in-scope, non-destructive conduct are the non-negotiable bounds separating testing from a crime."},
{q:"A penetration test is judged by whether it:",o:["Found the most vulnerabilities","Demonstrated real chained risk, stayed within authorization, and produced actionable business-risk reporting","Used the newest exploits","Ran the fastest"],a:1,x:"Demonstrated risk, safe/authorized conduct, and actionable reporting are the marks of a professional test that drives improvement."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A tester finds a reachable system that turns out to be outside the authorized scope. They should:",o:["Test it since it is reachable","Stop — testing outside scope is unauthorized access regardless of reachability","Report it as compromised","Exploit it quietly"],a:1,x:"Scope boundaries are sacred; reachability does not confer authorization, and testing out-of-scope systems is unauthorized access."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A penetration test proves a vulnerability by chaining it to reach the customer database, convincing leadership to fix it. This demonstrates the pentest value of:",o:["A long vulnerability list","Showing real business impact via exploitation chains — what a scan cannot convey","Automated scanning","Faster testing"],a:1,x:"Demonstrating the actual chain to a meaningful compromise conveys true risk that individual-vulnerability scanning cannot."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A tester considers running an exploit from an unknown source against production. The professional approach is:",o:["Run it immediately","Vet the exploit code and test its reliability in a lab first — never run unknown code blindly against a client","Skip exploitation","Run the most aggressive version"],a:1,x:"Untrusted exploit code can be malicious or unreliable; safe practice vets and tests before use to avoid harm and disruption."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A web test finds that changing a user ID in an API request returns another user's data. This is:",o:["A false positive","Broken access control — a serious, pervasive flaw requiring manual testing to confirm","Injection","Normal behavior"],a:1,x:"Access-control flaws (accessing what one should not) require human judgment to test and are among the most common serious web issues."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A tester harvests and easily cracks password hashes, then reuses one to move laterally. The findings map to which defenses?",o:["Physical locks","Strong/phishing-resistant MFA, credential protection, and strong hashing","Bigger disks","Faster networks"],a:1,x:"The credential attack validates identity weaknesses; the fixes are the BC-104 defenses that would have prevented the compromise."},
{q:"SCENARIO: From a web foothold, a tester escalates to admin, harvests a domain-admin credential, and reaches the domain controller. This demonstrates:",o:["A minor issue","Full domain compromise from a single web vulnerability — the demonstrated consequence that is the pentest's unique value","Nothing exploitable","A denial-of-service"],a:1,x:"Post-exploitation showing the chain from foothold to domain control demonstrates real impact mapping to segmentation/credential fixes."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A tester moves freely from a workstation to critical servers and pivots into a segmented network. This reveals:",o:["Strong segmentation","An under-segmented network — the exposure that turns footholds into total compromise; segmentation would break the movement","Good security","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Free lateral movement demonstrates inadequate segmentation; the finding maps directly to the containment defenses that would isolate systems."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A tester proves access to sensitive data. The responsible way to demonstrate it is:",o:["Exfiltrate all the data","Prove access with a screenshot or specific-file existence, without exfiltrating real sensitive data, per the ROE","Delete the data","Encrypt it"],a:1,x:"Demonstrating impact responsibly means proving access in a controlled, documented way — not causing harm or unnecessary exposure."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A report leads with technical jargon and CVSS scores, and leadership shelves it. The failure was:",o:["Too few findings","Not translating findings into business risk — leadership acts on mission risk, not jargon","Too much detail","No exploitation"],a:1,x:"The report must reach leadership in business-risk terms; a jargon dump gets shelved regardless of the findings' quality."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A penetration test is judged excellent. The correct standard is:",o:["It found the most CVEs","It demonstrated real chained risk, stayed within authorization and safe conduct, and reported actionable business risk","It used the newest exploits","It ran fastest"],a:1,x:"Demonstrated risk, safe/authorized conduct, and actionable reporting define a professional test that validates defenses and drives fixes."}
]};
